  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 158 AM MDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nMoisture has finally arrived over the Cowboy State, ending this\nprolonged streak of critical fire weather conditions. Partly to\nmostly cloudy skies continue over the state this morning, \nscattering out by midday day. Isolated showers to the west of \nPIH and SLC will continue to track eastward this morning and \nmove over far western portions of the forecast area around 10Z. \nVirga is likely from these showers, but amounts around 0.05\" \nwill be possible over the Tetons/Gros Ventres and toward \nUnion/Togwotee Pass. These showers may cross the Divide and move\nover the Upper Green River Basin, after sunrise this morning, \nbut will likely stay as virga. These showers are expected to end\nby 18Z, with dry conditions expected through the rest of the \nday. There is a 10% chance for virga showers over areas east of \nthe Divide late in the afternoon as a cold front drops \nsouthward. \n\nWidespread gusty winds will be the main concern again today, \nwith west wind gusts of 25 to 35 mph occurring across the CWA \nthis afternoon. The exception will be portions of the Bighorn \nBasin and the northern half of Johnson County. Northwest wind \ngusts up to 30 mph will occur in these areas as a cold front \nprogresses southward through the afternoon. The front will pass \nthrough the Bighorn Basin and Johnson County by 00Z Saturday \n(6pm), quickly reaching the Casper area and Riverton between 01Z\nand 03Z, eventually becoming stationary along the Divide by \n06Z. Temperatures will much cooler today, especially east of the\nDivide, running about 5 to 10 degrees cooler. This will apply \nto far northern portions, due to the proximity of the front. \n\nModels have become aligned with the much talked about storm \nsystem for this weekend, with not much change from yesterday \nwith the forecast track. The storm system is still forecasted to\ndrop southward along the West Coast tonight into Saturday. The \naforementioned cold front will gradually retreat northward \nSaturday afternoon, due to an increase in southwesterly winds. \nThe front looks to get pushed into the Bighorn Basin and further\nnorth into Sheridan County through Saturday afternoon. Apart \nfrom isolated showers and thunderstorms over eastern portions of\nSweetwater County Saturday afternoon, conditions are expected \nto remain dry. Precipitation chances continue to be pushed \nnorthward as the storm stays further north in MT. Little to no \nprecipitation is now expected from this storm. The associated \ncold front will push through the Cowboy State Sunday night, \nclearing the forecast area by 12Z Monday. Sub-freezing \ntemperatures will return to western portions Sunday night/Monday\nmorning, with readings in the upper 30s to lower 40s elsewhere.\nDry and cooler conditions will be in place Monday, with highs \nin the 60s to near 70 degrees across the area. Conditions will \nremain dry with seasonal temperatures Tuesday. A storm system \nmay impact the region Wednesday, but confidence is low at this \npoint as colder, Canadian airmasses begin to drop southward.",
